'''''Marsilea hirsuta''''', known as the '''nardoo''', is a species of aquatic [[fern]] in the family [[Marsileaceae]]. A widespread species with wide [[clover]] like fronds, found in flood plains and swamps in Australia. This plant was first mentioned in the scientific literature in 1810, in the ''[[Prodromus Florae Novae Hollandiae]]'', authored by Scottish botanist, [[Robert Brown (botanist, born 1773)|Robert Brown]]. One of the many plants listed with a [[Type (biology)#Type specimen|type]] as "(J.T.) v.v.".<ref name="RBGS">{{cite web |last1=Wilson |first1=Peter |title=''Marsilea hirsuta'' |url=http://plantnet.rbgsyd.nsw.gov.au/cgi-bin/NSWfl.pl?page=nswfl&lvl=sp&name=Marsilea~hirsuta |publisher=Royal Botanic Garden Sydney |access-date=1 June 2024}}</ref><ref>Les Robinson - Field Guide to the Native Plants of Sydney, {{ISBN|978-0-7318-1211-0}} page 433</ref>

